    {
      "paperId": "30869c857eaefe6859bc9606a0a3c5d48c7d5e36",
      "title": "A Transfer Learning Approach for Generalizing Reinforcement Learning in Autonomous Robot Navigation Using LiDAR: Cross-Platform Adaptation and Obstacle Avoidance",
      "abstract": "Cross-robot deployment of deep reinforcement learning (DRL) navigation remains difficult in dynamic environments because robot platforms often differ in embodiment, motion constraints, and LiDAR sensing geometry. This article proposes a transfer-learning (TL) framework for LiDAR-based autonomous navigation that reduces source–target observation mismatch through sensor-space alignment and stabilizes target adaptation through policy-preserving fine-tuning. To improve safety in dynamic environments, a lightweight cellular automata (CA)-based predictor is incorporated into the reinforcement-learning objective as a short-horizon, risk-aware shaping term. The proposed framework is validated through a multi-stage experimental protocol. First, a source policy is pretrained in a custom dynamic environment. Second, cross-platform transfer is evaluated in DynaBARN on three heterogeneous target robots: a quadruped, an omniwheel robot, and a differential-drive robot. Third, an additional benchmark across Easy, Medium, and Hard DynaBARN settings compares the proposed method against PPO+TL, DQN+TL, DWA, and TEB with statistical validation. Across the three target robots in DynaBARN, the proposed method achieves success rates of 81.0%–83.1%, collision rates of 5.4%–7.2%, and time-to-goal values of 35.6–39.7 s, yielding the best mean performance among the compared transfer-based baselines. In the difficulty-level benchmark, one-way ANOVA indicates significant group-level differences across metrics, and the proposed method degrades more gracefully than the compared methods while maintaining the best mean results. On a physical quadruped robot, the transferred policy achieves 90.0% success with 10.0% collision under controlled but non-identical obstacle interactions and real sensing/actuation uncertainty. These results indicate that combining sensor-space alignment, policy preservation, and short-horizon motion-risk shaping improves the robustness and practical deployability of LiDAR-based navigation across heterogeneous robots.",
